# ordered-disqualification-for-use-no-use-decisions

When deciding whether a container or lot is safe to use, evaluate disqualifiers in strict priority order from most severe to least severe, stopping and acting at the first disqualifier found. Do not proceed to lower-priority checks once a disqualifier is found. This prevents wasted effort on already-disqualified items and ensures the most serious issues (e.g., physical damage) are caught and acted on before subtler ones (e.g., use count).

## Procedures
- Evaluate disqualifiers in descending severity: physical damage (quarantine immediately) → flagged in inventory list (do not use) → condition-based threshold (e.g., use count, moisture exposure) → pass.
- At the first disqualifier, stop evaluation, apply the required action (quarantine, reject, clean), and do not advance to lower-priority checks.
- Document the disqualifier found and the action taken before selecting an alternative container.

## Conventions
- Damaged containers go directly to quarantine regardless of any other attribute.
- A flagged container is disqualified even if it appears undamaged.
- Condition-based thresholds (use count, bakeout state) are only evaluated if damage and flags are both clear.

